lessons.push({
  title: "Classes and Objects: Constructors, self and Instance Variables",
  minutes: 50,
  learn: ["Explain the idea of object-oriented programming", "Define a class and create objects from it", "Write __init__ and use self", "Give objects a readable text form with __repr__ and __str__"],
  why: "Real programs model things: students, accounts, products. A class bundles the data of a thing with the operations on it, so code mirrors the problem and large programs stay manageable.",
  realWorld: "A banking app has thousands of <code>BankAccount</code> objects, each with its own owner and balance but all behaving the same way.",
  simple: "A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint. An <strong>object</strong> (instance) is one thing built from it. <code>__init__</code> sets up each new object; <code>self</code> means &quot;this particular object&quot;.",
  detail: `<h3>Procedural vs object-oriented</h3>
<p>So far, data (variables, lists) and behaviour (functions) were separate. OOP keeps the data and the functions that work on it together in one unit, the object. This helps hide details, avoid global variables and reuse code.</p>
<h3>Parts</h3>
${table(["Term", "Meaning", "Example"], [["class", "blueprint", "<code>class BankAccount:</code>"], ["object / instance", "one thing built from the class", "<code>acc = BankAccount(\"Asha\", 500)</code>"], ["attribute", "data stored on an object", "<code>acc.balance</code>"], ["method", "function defined in the class", "<code>acc.deposit(100)</code>"], ["constructor", "<code>__init__</code>, runs when an object is created", "sets the starting attributes"], ["self", "first parameter of every instance method: the object itself", "Python passes it automatically"]])}
<h3>How a method call works</h3>
<p><code>acc.deposit(100)</code> is the same as <code>BankAccount.deposit(acc, 100)</code>: Python fills in <code>self</code> with <code>acc</code>. That is why each object keeps its own data.</p>
<h3>Representing objects as text</h3>
<ul><li><code>__repr__</code> returns an unambiguous developer view, shown in the shell and in lists. Make it look like the code that would rebuild the object.</li><li><code>__str__</code> returns the friendly text used by <code>print()</code> and f-strings. If missing, Python uses <code>__repr__</code>.</li></ul>
<h3>Naming</h3>
<p>Classes use <code>CapWords</code> (<code>StudentRecord</code>), methods and attributes use <code>snake_case</code>.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `class Name:\n    def __init__(self, a, b):\n        self.a = a\n        self.b = b\n    def method(self, x):\n        return self.a + x\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f"Name({self.a!r}, {self.b!r})"`, note: "Create an object by calling the class: Name(1, 2)." },
  diagram: graph("A class and its objects", "One class BankAccount as a blueprint with two objects built from it, each with its own owner and balance.", 640, 210,
    [{ id: "c", k: "term", t: "class BankAccount\n(blueprint)", x: 320, y: 40, w: 220, h: 54 }, { id: "a", k: "proc", t: "acc1\nowner Asha\nbalance 500", x: 160, y: 165, w: 170, h: 66 }, { id: "b", k: "proc", t: "acc2\nowner Ravi\nbalance 120", x: 480, y: 165, w: 170, h: 66 }],
    [{ from: "c", to: "a", label: "creates" }, { from: "c", to: "b", label: "creates" }]),
  example: { code: `class BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):\n        self.owner = owner\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self.balance += amount\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if amount > self.balance:\n            return False\n        self.balance -= amount\n        return True\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f"BankAccount({self.owner!r}, {self.balance})"\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f"{self.owner}: Rs {self.balance}"\n\nacc1 = BankAccount("Asha", 500)\nacc2 = BankAccount("Ravi")\nacc1.deposit(250)\nprint(acc1.withdraw(2000), acc1.withdraw(100))\nprint(acc1)\nprint(repr(acc2))\nprint(acc1.balance, acc2.balance)\nprint(isinstance(acc1, BankAccount), type(acc1).__name__)`, out: `False True\nAsha: Rs 650\nBankAccount('Ravi', 0)\n650 0\nTrue BankAccount` },
  lineByLine: [["def __init__(self, owner, balance=0)", "Runs when <code>BankAccount(...)</code> is called; stores the starting data on the new object."], ["self.balance += amount", "Changes only this object's balance."], ["return False / return True", "withdraw reports whether it worked."], ["__repr__ and __str__", "Developer text and user text."], ["acc1.balance, acc2.balance", "The objects have separate data."], ["isinstance(acc1, BankAccount)", "True: acc1 is an instance of the class."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Tracing <code>acc1</code>:", headers: ["Statement", "acc1.balance", "Result"], rows: [["BankAccount(\"Asha\", 500)", "500", "object created"], ["deposit(250)", "750", "-"], ["withdraw(2000)", "750", "False (too large)"], ["withdraw(100)", "650", "True"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Two students, each an object with its own data, in a list.", code: `class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name, marks):\n        self.name = name\n        self.marks = marks\n\n    def average(self):\n        return sum(self.marks) / len(self.marks)\n\nclass_list = [Student("Asha", [80, 90]), Student("Ravi", [60, 75, 90])]\nfor s in class_list:\n    print(s.name, round(s.average(), 1))\nprint(max(class_list, key=lambda s: s.average()).name)`, out: `Asha 85.0\nRavi 75.0\nAsha` },
  mistakes: ["Forgetting <code>self</code> as the first parameter.", "Forgetting <code>self.</code> before an attribute (it becomes a local variable).", "Calling a method on the class without an object.", "Mutable default arguments in <code>__init__</code> (<code>marks=[]</code>).", "Putting printing inside methods that should return values."],
  practices: ["Initialise every attribute in <code>__init__</code>.", "Always give your classes a <code>__repr__</code>.", "Keep each class focused on one thing.", "Methods that compute should return; let the caller print."],
  interview: ["What is the difference between a class and an object?", "What does self refer to?", "What does __init__ do? Is it a constructor?", "Difference between __str__ and __repr__?"],
  practiceQs: ["Write a Rectangle class with area() and perimeter().", "Why is self.name = name needed in __init__?", "What does print(obj) show if neither __str__ nor __repr__ is defined?", "Create three Student objects and find the best."],
  revision: ["class = blueprint, object = instance.", "__init__ initialises, self is this object.", "__repr__ for developers, __str__ for users.", "Each object has its own attributes."],
  hinglish: "Class ek blueprint hai aur object uska ek asli roop. __init__ naye object ko shuru karta hai aur self us object ko batata hai. Har object ka apna data hota hai.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What does self refer to in a method?", ["The class", "The object the method is called on", "The module", "The parent class"], 1, "Python passes the instance automatically."),
    mcq("When does __init__ run?", ["When the class is defined", "When an object is created", "When the program ends", "When a method is called"], 1, "Calling the class creates an instance and runs __init__."),
    mcq("What is printed?\n\nclass A:\n    def __init__(self, v):\n        self.v = v\na = A(3)\nb = A(5)\nprint(a.v, b.v)", ["3 5", "5 5", "3 3", "Error"], 0, "Each object has its own attribute.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("Find the bug:\n\nclass C:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        n = n", ["self.n is never set", "Missing colon", "n must be a string", "No bug"], 0, "The value is assigned to a local variable, not to the object.", "DEBUG"),
    mcq("Which is used by print() if both exist?", ["__repr__", "__str__", "__init__", "__call__"], 1, "print uses __str__ first.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Bank account commands", "Read the opening balance on the first line, then lines of commands until the input ends: deposit N or withdraw N. Use a BankAccount class. A withdrawal larger than the balance prints Insufficient funds and changes nothing. At the end print Balance: followed by the balance.", "import sys\n\nclass BankAccount:\n    pass\n\nlines = sys.stdin.read().split(\"\\n\")\n# first line: opening balance; then commands\n", `import sys\n\nclass BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self.balance += amount\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if amount > self.balance:\n            return False\n        self.balance -= amount\n        return True\n\nlines = sys.stdin.read().split("\\n")\naccount = BankAccount(int(lines[0]))\nfor line in lines[1:]:\n    if not line.strip():\n        continue\n    action, amount = line.split()\n    amount = int(amount)\n    if action == "deposit":\n        account.deposit(amount)\n    elif not account.withdraw(amount):\n        print("Insufficient funds")\nprint("Balance:", account.balance)`, [["100\ndeposit 50\nwithdraw 500\nwithdraw 30", "Insufficient funds\nBalance: 120"], ["10", "Balance: 10"]], [["100\ndeposit 50\nwithdraw 500\nwithdraw 30", "Insufficient funds\nBalance: 120"], ["10", "Balance: 10"], ["0\nwithdraw 1", "Insufficient funds\nBalance: 0"], ["500\nwithdraw 500\ndeposit 5", "Balance: 5"]])],
});

