lessons.push({
  title: "Class Variables, Instance, Class and Static Methods",
  minutes: 45,
  learn: ["Tell instance variables from class variables", "Write instance, class and static methods", "Use @classmethod as an alternative constructor", "Avoid the shared-state surprises of class variables"],
  why: "Some data belongs to every object of a type (a tax rate, a count of objects), and some helpers do not need an object at all. Python has a tool for each case.",
  realWorld: "<code>Student.count</code> counts all students created; <code>Date.from_string(\"2026-11-02\")</code> builds a date from text; a tax rate is shared by every invoice.",
  simple: "<strong>Instance variables</strong> belong to one object. <strong>Class variables</strong> are shared by all objects of the class. <code>@classmethod</code> receives the class; <code>@staticmethod</code> receives nothing extra.",
  detail: `${table(["", "Instance method", "Class method", "Static method"], [["Decorator", "none", "<code>@classmethod</code>", "<code>@staticmethod</code>"], ["First parameter", "<code>self</code> (the object)", "<code>cls</code> (the class)", "none"], ["Can use instance data", "yes", "no", "no"], ["Can use class data", "yes (via <code>self</code> or the class)", "yes", "only by naming the class"], ["Typical use", "normal behaviour", "alternative constructors, factory methods, class-wide data", "utility that relates to the class but needs no state"]])}
<h3>Class variables</h3>
<p>Defined in the class body, outside any method. Read them through the class (<code>Student.count</code>) or an object. <strong>Assigning</strong> through an object (<code>obj.count = 5</code>) creates a new instance variable that hides the class one: change shared data through the class name. A <em>mutable</em> class variable such as a list is shared by every object, which is rarely what you want for per-object data.</p>
<h3>Alternative constructors</h3>
<p>Python has one <code>__init__</code>, so use class methods for other ways to build objects: <code>Temperature.from_fahrenheit(212)</code>. Because they receive <code>cls</code>, they also work for subclasses.</p>
<h3>Static methods</h3>
<p>A plain function that logically belongs in the class namespace, for example validation: <code>Account.is_valid_number("123")</code>. If it does not need the class, a module-level function is often better.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `class Student:\n    count = 0                     # class variable\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name          # instance variable\n        Student.count += 1\n    @classmethod\n    def from_csv(cls, text):\n        return cls(text.split(",")[0])\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_valid(name):\n        return name.isalpha()`, note: "Change shared data through the class name." },
  diagram: graph("Where each kind of data lives", "The class holds the class variable count shared by all; each object holds its own name.", 620, 210,
    [{ id: "c", k: "term", t: "class Student\ncount = 3  (shared)", x: 310, y: 45, w: 250, h: 58 }, { id: "a", k: "proc", t: "name = Asha", x: 110, y: 160, w: 150, h: 40 }, { id: "b", k: "proc", t: "name = Ravi", x: 310, y: 160, w: 150, h: 40 }, { id: "z", k: "proc", t: "name = Zoya", x: 510, y: 160, w: 150, h: 40 }],
    [{ from: "c", to: "a" }, { from: "c", to: "b" }, { from: "c", to: "z" }]),
  example: { code: `class Temperature:\n    scale = "Celsius"\n    count = 0\n\n    def __init__(self, celsius):\n        self.celsius = celsius\n        Temperature.count += 1\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_fahrenheit(cls, f):\n        return cls(round((f - 32) * 5 / 9, 1))\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_valid(value):\n        return value >= -273.15\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f"Temperature({self.celsius})"\n\na = Temperature(25)\nb = Temperature.from_fahrenheit(212)\nprint(a, b, Temperature.count)\nprint(Temperature.is_valid(-300), a.scale)\na.scale = "Kelvin"\nprint(a.scale, b.scale, Temperature.scale)\nclass Basket:\n    items = []\n    def add(self, x):\n        self.items.append(x)\nb1, b2 = Basket(), Basket()\nb1.add("pen")\nprint(b2.items)`, out: `Temperature(25) Temperature(100.0) 2\nFalse Celsius\nKelvin Celsius Celsius\n['pen']` },
  lineByLine: [["count = 0 and Temperature.count += 1", "One counter shared by all objects."], ["@classmethod from_fahrenheit(cls, f)", "A second way to build a Temperature; <code>cls(...)</code> calls the constructor."], ["@staticmethod is_valid(value)", "No self and no cls: a helper in the class namespace."], ["a.scale = \"Kelvin\"", "Creates an <em>instance</em> variable on a only; b and the class are unchanged."], ["items = [] in Basket", "A mutable class variable is shared: b2 sees what b1 added."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Creating objects:", headers: ["Statement", "Temperature.count"], rows: [["a = Temperature(25)", "1"], ["b = Temperature.from_fahrenheit(212)", "2 (the class method calls the constructor)"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "An alternative constructor that parses text, and a counter of created objects.", code: `class Student:\n    count = 0\n    def __init__(self, name, marks):\n        self.name, self.marks = name, marks\n        Student.count += 1\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_line(cls, line):\n        name, *marks = line.split()\n        return cls(name, [int(m) for m in marks])\n\ns = Student.from_line("Asha 80 90 70")\nprint(s.name, s.marks, Student.count)`, out: `Asha [80, 90, 70] 1` },
  mistakes: ["Using a list or dictionary as a class variable for per-object data.", "Changing a class variable through an object and creating a hidden instance variable.", "Forgetting <code>@classmethod</code> and then missing <code>cls</code>.", "Making everything static: that is just functions in a class.", "Using <code>self</code> in a static method."],
  practices: ["Instance variables for object data; class variables for constants and counters.", "Use class methods for alternative constructors.", "Prefer module-level functions over static methods unless the grouping helps."],
  interview: ["Instance vs class vs static method?", "What is the difference between a class variable and an instance variable?", "Why use a class method as a constructor?", "What happens on obj.class_var = value?"],
  practiceQs: ["Add a class variable tax_rate = 0.18 to an Invoice class.", "Create from_string for a Date class.", "Why do b1 and b2 share items in the Basket example?", "How do you fix Basket?"],
  revision: ["Instance data: self.x. Class data: shared.", "@classmethod gets cls; @staticmethod gets nothing.", "Alternative constructors via classmethods.", "Do not use mutable class variables for per-object data."],
  hinglish: "Instance variable har object ka apna hota hai, class variable sab objects mein shared. @classmethod ko class (cls) milti hai, isse doosra constructor banate hain. @staticmethod ko kuch nahi milta.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("Which receives the class as its first argument?", ["instance method", "@classmethod", "@staticmethod", "__init__"], 1, "A class method receives cls."),
    mcq("What is printed?\n\nclass A:\n    n = 0\n    def __init__(self):\n        A.n += 1\nA(); A()\nprint(A.n)", ["0", "1", "2", "Error"], 2, "The shared counter was incremented twice.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What does a.x = 5 do if x is a class variable and a an object?", ["Changes it for every object", "Creates an instance variable on a", "Raises an error", "Deletes the class variable"], 1, "It shadows the class variable for that object only."),
    mcq("Which method type cannot access self?", ["instance", "class", "static", "special"], 2, "Static methods get no automatic first argument."),
    mcq("Why is a list class variable dangerous?", ["It is slow", "All objects share the same list", "It is read-only", "It cannot be created"], 1, "Mutating it affects everyone.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Fahrenheit constructor", "Define a Temperature class with a class method from_fahrenheit(f) that stores the Celsius value (f - 32) * 5 / 9. Read a Fahrenheit value and print the Celsius value rounded to 1 decimal.", "class Temperature:\n    def __init__(self, celsius):\n        self.celsius = celsius\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_fahrenheit(cls, f):\n        pass\n\nf = float(input())\n# print the celsius value rounded to 1 decimal\n", `class Temperature:\n    def __init__(self, celsius):\n        self.celsius = celsius\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_fahrenheit(cls, f):\n        return cls((f - 32) * 5 / 9)\n\nf = float(input())\nprint(round(Temperature.from_fahrenheit(f).celsius, 1))`, [["212", "100.0"], ["32", "0.0"]], [["212", "100.0"], ["32", "0.0"], ["98.6", "37.0"], ["-40", "-40.0"], ["0", "-17.8"]])],
});

