lessons.push({
  title: "Encapsulation, Properties and Getters/Setters",
  minutes: 40,
  learn: ["Protect an object's data and keep it valid", "Use naming conventions for private data", "Write @property getters and setters", "Know what name mangling does and does not do"],
  why: "If anyone can set <code>account.balance = -1000000</code>, the program cannot be trusted. Encapsulation lets a class guard its own rules.",
  realWorld: "A <code>Temperature</code> must not go below absolute zero; an <code>Employee</code>'s salary must be positive. The class checks every change.",
  simple: "<strong>Encapsulation</strong> means keeping data and the code that guards it together, and letting outsiders use only the safe interface. In Python a leading underscore says &quot;internal&quot;, and <code>@property</code> lets a method look like a plain attribute while still validating.",
  detail: `<h3>Conventions, not locks</h3>
${table(["Name", "Meaning"], [["<code>name</code>", "public: part of the interface"], ["<code>_name</code>", "&quot;protected&quot;/internal by convention: do not use from outside"], ["<code>__name</code>", "name-mangled to <code>_ClassName__name</code>: avoids accidental clashes in subclasses; still reachable, so it is <em>not</em> real privacy"]])}
<p>Python trusts programmers (&quot;we are all consenting adults&quot;). Privacy is a promise, not enforcement.</p>
<h3>Properties</h3>
<p><code>@property</code> turns a method into a read-only attribute; add <code>@name.setter</code> to allow assignment with validation. Callers keep writing <code>obj.salary = 5000</code>, so you can start with a plain attribute and add validation later without changing any caller. Do not write Java-style <code>get_x()</code>/<code>set_x()</code> methods unless needed.</p>
<h3>Computed attributes</h3>
<p>A property can compute a value on demand (<code>area</code> from <code>width</code> and <code>height</code>), so it can never be out of date.</p>
<h3>Read-only attributes</h3>
<p>A property with no setter raises <code>AttributeError</code> on assignment.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `class Account:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self._balance = balance\n    @property\n    def balance(self):\n        return self._balance\n    @balance.setter\n    def balance(self, value):\n        if value < 0:\n            raise ValueError("negative")\n        self._balance = value`, note: "Same attribute syntax, validated behind the scenes." },
  diagram: graph("A property guards an attribute", "Outside code reads or writes obj.balance; the property setter validates and then stores the value in the internal attribute _balance.", 640, 150,
    [{ id: "o", k: "term", t: "outside code\nobj.balance = 50", x: 100, y: 75, w: 160, h: 56 }, { id: "p", k: "dec", t: "setter:\nvalid?", x: 320, y: 75, w: 130, h: 64 }, { id: "s", k: "proc", t: "self._balance\n= 50", x: 540, y: 45, w: 150, h: 46 }, { id: "e", k: "io", t: "raise ValueError", x: 540, y: 115, w: 150, h: 36 }],
    [{ from: "o", to: "p" }, { from: "p", to: "s", label: "yes" }, { from: "p", to: "e", label: "no" }]),
  example: { code: `class Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self.width = width\n        self.height = height\n\n    @property\n    def width(self):\n        return self._width\n\n    @width.setter\n    def width(self, value):\n        if value <= 0:\n            raise ValueError("width must be positive")\n        self._width = value\n\n    @property\n    def area(self):\n        return self._width * self.height\n\nr = Rectangle(4, 5)\nprint(r.width, r.area)\nr.width = 10\nprint(r.area)\ntry:\n    r.width = -1\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print("rejected:", error)\ntry:\n    r.area = 99\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print("area is read-only")\n\nclass Secret:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__code = 123\nprint(Secret()._Secret__code)`, out: `4 20\n50\nrejected: width must be positive\narea is read-only\n123` },
  lineByLine: [["self.width = width in __init__", "Goes through the setter, so even the starting value is validated."], ["@property def width", "Reading <code>r.width</code> calls this method."], ["@width.setter", "Assigning <code>r.width = ...</code> calls this method."], ["@property def area", "Computed on demand, read-only."], ["self.__code", "Mangled to <code>_Secret__code</code>; still reachable, so not truly private."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "<code>r.width = -1</code>:", headers: ["Step", "What happens"], rows: [["assignment", "Python calls the width setter with value = -1"], ["value <= 0", "True"], ["raise ValueError", "attribute unchanged; caller sees the error"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A BankAccount that cannot be put into an invalid state from outside.", code: `class BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, balance=0):\n        self._balance = balance\n\n    @property\n    def balance(self):\n        return self._balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError("deposit must be positive")\n        self._balance += amount\n\nacc = BankAccount(100)\nacc.deposit(50)\nprint(acc.balance)\ntry:\n    acc.balance = 0\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print("use deposit() instead")`, out: `150\nuse deposit() instead` },
  mistakes: ["Setting <code>self._width</code> in <code>__init__</code> and bypassing validation.", "Defining the setter without the property first (or with a different name).", "Believing double underscores make data private.", "Writing get_/set_ methods for every attribute.", "Infinite recursion: the property setter assigns to <code>self.width</code> instead of <code>self._width</code>."],
  practices: ["Start with plain public attributes; add a property when you need validation.", "Validate in the setter and in <code>__init__</code> (through the setter).", "Use computed properties for derived values."],
  interview: ["What is encapsulation?", "How does Python implement private attributes?", "What does @property do?", "Why prefer properties over getters and setters?"],
  practiceQs: ["Add a read-only diameter property to a Circle with a radius.", "Make age reject negative values.", "What does _x versus __x signal?", "What happens if you assign to a read-only property?"],
  revision: ["_name internal; __name mangled.", "@property getter, @x.setter setter.", "Validation lives in the setter.", "Read-only = property without a setter."],
  hinglish: "Encapsulation matlab data ko class ke andar surakshit rakhna. _naam ka matlab hai ki bahar se mat chhedo. @property se method ko attribute ki tarah use kar sakte hain aur setter mein value check kar sakte hain.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What does a single leading underscore signal?", ["The attribute is truly private", "The attribute is internal by convention", "It is a constant", "It is deleted"], 1, "It is a convention, not enforcement."),
    mcq("What happens when you assign to a property that has no setter?", ["It works", "AttributeError", "ValueError", "It is ignored"], 1, "Read-only properties reject assignment.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What does @property allow?", ["Calling a method like an attribute", "Creating a class variable", "Making a static method", "Multiple inheritance"], 0, "Attribute syntax, method behaviour."),
    mcq("What does self.__x inside class A become?", ["_A__x", "__A_x", "x", "A.__x"], 0, "Name mangling prefixes the class name."),
    mcq("What is wrong?\n\n@width.setter\ndef width(self, v):\n    self.width = v", ["Infinite recursion", "Missing return", "Nothing", "Wrong decorator"], 0, "It must store into self._width.", "DEBUG")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Validated rectangle", "Define a Rectangle class whose width and height properties reject values that are zero or negative by raising ValueError. Read the width and the height (one per line). If either is invalid print Invalid, otherwise print the area.", "class Rectangle:\n    pass\n\nw = int(input())\nh = int(input())\n# build a Rectangle inside try/except and print the area or Invalid\n", `class Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, width, height):\n        self.width = width\n        self.height = height\n\n    @property\n    def width(self):\n        return self._width\n\n    @width.setter\n    def width(self, value):\n        if value <= 0:\n            raise ValueError("width")\n        self._width = value\n\n    @property\n    def height(self):\n        return self._height\n\n    @height.setter\n    def height(self, value):\n        if value <= 0:\n            raise ValueError("height")\n        self._height = value\n\n    @property\n    def area(self):\n        return self.width * self.height\n\nw = int(input())\nh = int(input())\ntry:\n    print(Rectangle(w, h).area)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Invalid")`, [["4\n5", "20"], ["-1\n5", "Invalid"]], [["4\n5", "20"], ["-1\n5", "Invalid"], ["3\n0", "Invalid"], ["7\n7", "49"], ["1\n1", "1"]])],
});

