lessons.push({
  title: "Multiple Inheritance, Method Resolution Order and Mixins",
  minutes: 40,
  learn: ["Create a class with several parents", "Predict the method lookup order (MRO)", "Understand the diamond problem and cooperative super()", "Use mixins responsibly"],
  why: "Libraries and frameworks use multiple inheritance (mixins). Understanding the MRO is also a favourite interview topic.",
  realWorld: "A <code>SavingsAccount(InterestMixin, Account)</code> mixes in interest behaviour; Django class-based views combine several mixins.",
  simple: "A class can have more than one parent: <code>class D(B, C)</code>. When the same method exists in several parents, Python follows a fixed order, the <strong>MRO</strong>, that you can print with <code>D.__mro__</code>.",
  detail: `<h3>The diamond</h3>
<p>If B and C both inherit from A and D inherits from B and C, A is reachable by two paths. Python uses the <strong>C3 linearisation</strong> to make one consistent order: children before parents, parents in the order written, and each class only once.</p>
<p>For <code>class D(B, C)</code> with B and C inheriting A the MRO is <code>D, B, C, A, object</code>.</p>
<h3>Cooperative super()</h3>
<p><code>super()</code> does not mean &quot;my parent&quot;: it means &quot;the next class in the MRO of the <em>object's</em> class&quot;. When every class in the chain calls <code>super().method()</code>, each runs exactly once. If one class in the chain forgets, the rest are skipped.</p>
<h3>Mixins</h3>
<p>A mixin is a small class that adds one capability (logging, JSON export, comparison) and is not meant to stand alone. Keep mixins without their own state, name them <code>...Mixin</code>, and list them <em>before</em> the main base class.</p>
<h3>Avoid complexity</h3>
<p>Prefer composition (hold another object) when you only need to reuse behaviour. Use multiple inheritance for genuine mixins and framework patterns. If the MRO cannot be built (conflicting orders), Python raises <code>TypeError</code>.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `class D(B, C):\n    ...\nD.__mro__\nD.mro()\nsuper().method()   # next in the MRO`, note: "The order of the parents matters." },
  diagram: graph("The diamond and its MRO", "A at the top, B and C below it, D at the bottom inheriting from B and C; the lookup order is D, B, C, A.", 600, 270,
    [{ id: "a", k: "term", t: "A", x: 300, y: 36, w: 90, h: 38 }, { id: "b", k: "proc", t: "B(A)", x: 160, y: 130, w: 100, h: 40 }, { id: "c", k: "proc", t: "C(A)", x: 440, y: 130, w: 100, h: 40 }, { id: "d", k: "proc", t: "D(B, C)", x: 300, y: 225, w: 120, h: 40 }],
    [{ from: "b", to: "a" }, { from: "c", to: "a" }, { from: "d", to: "b" }, { from: "d", to: "c" }]),
  example: { code: `class A:\n    def who(self):\n        return ["A"]\n\nclass B(A):\n    def who(self):\n        return ["B"] + super().who()\n\nclass C(A):\n    def who(self):\n        return ["C"] + super().who()\n\nclass D(B, C):\n    def who(self):\n        return ["D"] + super().who()\n\nprint(D().who())\nprint([k.__name__ for k in D.__mro__])\n\nclass JsonMixin:\n    def to_json(self):\n        import json\n        return json.dumps(self.__dict__, sort_keys=True)\n\nclass Point(JsonMixin):\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\n\nprint(Point(1, 2).to_json())\ntry:\n    class Bad(A, B):\n        pass\nexcept TypeError:\n    print("inconsistent MRO")`, out: `['D', 'B', 'C', 'A']\n['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object']\n{"x": 1, "y": 2}\ninconsistent MRO` },
  lineByLine: [["class D(B, C)", "Parents are searched in this order."], ["super().who() in B", "From a D object, the next class after B in the MRO is C, not A."], ["D.__mro__", "The complete lookup order, ending in object."], ["JsonMixin", "Adds <code>to_json</code> to any class that lists it."], ["class Bad(A, B)", "A comes before its own child B: no consistent order exists."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "How <code>D().who()</code> runs:", headers: ["Class", "Adds", "Calls next in MRO"], rows: [["D", "'D'", "B"], ["B", "'B'", "C (not A!)"], ["C", "'C'", "A"], ["A", "'A'", "-"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A logging mixin used with an ordinary class.", code: `class LogMixin:\n    def log(self, message):\n        return f"[{type(self).__name__}] {message}"\n\nclass Account:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.balance = balance\n\nclass SavingsAccount(LogMixin, Account):\n    def add_interest(self, rate):\n        self.balance += self.balance * rate // 100\n        return self.log(f"balance is {self.balance}")\n\nprint(SavingsAccount(1000).add_interest(5))\nprint([c.__name__ for c in SavingsAccount.__mro__])`, out: `[SavingsAccount] balance is 1050\n['SavingsAccount', 'LogMixin', 'Account', 'object']` },
  mistakes: ["Assuming super() always means the direct parent.", "Forgetting to call <code>super()</code> in one class of a cooperative chain.", "Mixins with their own __init__ and state.", "Listing the base class before the mixin and losing the override.", "Creating tangled hierarchies instead of using composition."],
  practices: ["Print <code>Class.__mro__</code> when in doubt.", "Make mixins small and stateless.", "Use composition by default."],
  interview: ["What is the diamond problem and how does Python solve it?", "What is MRO?", "What does super() really do?", "What is a mixin?"],
  practiceQs: ["Predict the MRO of class X(A, B) where A and B inherit from object.", "Why does each who() run only once?", "Write a mixin that adds a describe() method.", "How can you read the MRO of any class?"],
  revision: ["class C(A, B): parents left to right.", "C3 gives one consistent order.", "super() = next in the MRO.", "Mixin = small, stateless capability class."],
  hinglish: "Python mein ek class ke kai parent ho sakte hain. Method kahan se milega, ye MRO tay karta hai (D, B, C, A). super() ka matlab 'agla class MRO mein' hai, seedha parent nahi.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What is the MRO of class D(B, C) when B and C both inherit A?", ["D, A, B, C", "D, B, C, A", "D, B, A, C", "A, B, C, D"], 1, "Children first, parents in written order, A last."),
    mcq("What does super() refer to in multiple inheritance?", ["The first parent", "The next class in the MRO", "The last parent", "object always"], 1, "It follows the MRO of the instance's class."),
    mcq("Where can you see the MRO?", ["ClassName.__mro__", "ClassName.__parents__", "dir(Class)[0]", "ClassName.super"], 0, "__mro__ is a tuple of classes."),
    mcq("What is a good mixin?", ["A large class with state", "A small class adding one capability", "A function", "An abstract database"], 1, "Mixins are small and focused."),
    mcq("What happens on class Bad(A, B) if B inherits from A?", ["Works fine", "TypeError: cannot create a consistent MRO", "A is ignored", "B is ignored"], 1, "The parent order contradicts the hierarchy.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Diamond order", "Define classes A, B(A), C(A) and D(B, C). Print the names of the classes in the MRO of D, excluding object, separated by spaces.", "class A:\n    pass\n\n# define B, C and D and print the MRO names\n", `class A:\n    pass\n\nclass B(A):\n    pass\n\nclass C(A):\n    pass\n\nclass D(B, C):\n    pass\n\nprint(" ".join(k.__name__ for k in D.__mro__[:-1]))`, [["", "D B C A"], ["", "D B C A"]], [["", "D B C A"]])],
});

