lessons.push({
  title: "Polymorphism, Magic Methods and Operator Overloading",
  minutes: 50,
  learn: ["Explain polymorphism and duck typing", "Implement special methods such as __len__, __eq__, __lt__, __add__", "Overload operators for your own classes", "Emulate overloading with default arguments and singledispatch"],
  why: "Magic (dunder) methods make your objects behave like built-in types: you can add them, compare them, sort them and loop over them, using the same natural syntax.",
  realWorld: "An Online Shopping <code>Cart</code> supports <code>len(cart)</code> and <code>cart + item</code>; a <code>Money</code> class supports addition and comparison; a <code>Vector</code> supports <code>v1 + v2</code>.",
  simple: "<strong>Polymorphism</strong> means &quot;many forms&quot;: the same call works on different kinds of objects, each doing its own thing. Special methods named <code>__like_this__</code> tell Python how your object should respond to operators and built-in functions.",
  detail: `<h3>Polymorphism and duck typing</h3>
<p>Python does not check types in advance: if an object has the method you call, it works (&quot;if it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck&quot;). A function that calls <code>shape.area()</code> accepts a Circle, a Rectangle or any future shape.</p>
<h3>Common special methods</h3>
${table(["Method", "Called by", "Purpose"], [["<code>__repr__</code>, <code>__str__</code>", "repr(), print()", "text forms"], ["<code>__len__</code>", "len(x)", "size"], ["<code>__getitem__</code>", "x[i]", "indexing, and iteration if no __iter__"], ["<code>__iter__</code>, <code>__next__</code>", "for loops", "iteration (Module 11)"], ["<code>__contains__</code>", "item in x", "membership"], ["<code>__eq__</code>, <code>__lt__</code> ...", "==, &lt; ...", "comparison and sorting"], ["<code>__add__</code>, <code>__sub__</code>, <code>__mul__</code>", "+, -, *", "arithmetic"], ["<code>__bool__</code>", "if x:", "truth value"], ["<code>__call__</code>", "x()", "make an object callable"], ["<code>__enter__</code>, <code>__exit__</code>", "with", "context managers"]])}
<h3>Rules</h3>
<ul><li>Return a <strong>new</strong> object from arithmetic methods; do not change the operands.</li><li>If you do not support the other operand's type, return <code>NotImplemented</code> (not raise), so Python can try the other side.</li><li>Defining <code>__eq__</code> without <code>__hash__</code> makes the class unhashable (not usable in sets or as dict keys). Define both if instances should be hashable.</li><li><code>functools.total_ordering</code> fills in the other comparisons if you define <code>__eq__</code> and one of <code>__lt__</code>, <code>__le__</code>, <code>__gt__</code>, <code>__ge__</code>.</li></ul>
<h3>Method overloading in Python</h3>
<p>Python does not choose between several methods of the same name by parameter types. Alternatives: default and keyword arguments, <code>*args</code>, checking <code>isinstance</code>, or <code>functools.singledispatch</code> (dispatch on the type of the first argument).</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `class Vector:\n    def __init__(self, x, y): ...\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n    def __eq__(self, other): ...\n    def __lt__(self, other): ...\n    def __len__(self): ...`, note: "Operators call special methods." },
  diagram: chain("What v1 + v2 really does", "Left to right: the expression v1 + v2, Python calls v1.__add__(v2), which builds and returns a new Vector.",
    [{ k: "io", t: "v1 + v2" }, { k: "proc", t: "Python calls\nv1.__add__(v2)" }, { k: "proc", t: "builds a new\nVector" }, { k: "io", t: "result" }], { w: 150, gap: 30, hgt: 60 }),
  example: { code: `from functools import total_ordering, singledispatch\n\n@total_ordering\nclass Money:\n    def __init__(self, rupees):\n        self.rupees = rupees\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        if not isinstance(other, Money):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return Money(self.rupees + other.rupees)\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return isinstance(other, Money) and self.rupees == other.rupees\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        return self.rupees < other.rupees\n    def __hash__(self):\n        return hash(self.rupees)\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f"Money({self.rupees})"\n\nprint(Money(5) + Money(7), Money(5) < Money(7), Money(9) >= Money(9))\nprint(sorted([Money(30), Money(10), Money(20)]))\nprint(len({Money(1), Money(1), Money(2)}))\n\nclass Cart:\n    def __init__(self, *items):\n        self.items = list(items)\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.items)\n    def __getitem__(self, index):\n        return self.items[index]\n    def __contains__(self, item):\n        return item in self.items\n    def __call__(self, extra):\n        return Cart(*self.items, extra)\n\ncart = Cart("pen", "book")\nprint(len(cart), cart[0], "book" in cart, [i for i in cart(\"bag\")])\n\n@singledispatch\ndef show(value):\n    return "object"\n@show.register\ndef _(value: int):\n    return "integer"\n@show.register\ndef _(value: str):\n    return "text"\nprint(show(3), show("x"), show(2.5))`, out: `Money(12) True True\n[Money(10), Money(20), Money(30)]\n2\n2 pen True ['pen', 'book', 'bag']\ninteger text object` },
  lineByLine: [["return NotImplemented", "Tells Python this operand type is unsupported, so it can try the other side or raise TypeError."], ["__eq__ and __hash__", "Equal Money objects hash equally, so they work in sets."], ["@total_ordering", "Generates <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code> from <code>==</code> and <code>&lt;</code>."], ["sorted([...])", "Sorting uses __lt__."], ["__len__, __getitem__, __contains__", "Cart now works with len(), indexing, <code>in</code> and loops."], ["__call__", "An object that can be used like a function."], ["@singledispatch", "Chooses the implementation by the type of the first argument."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Evaluating <code>Money(5) + Money(7)</code>:", headers: ["Step", "What happens"], rows: [["1", "Python looks for Money.__add__"], ["2", "Calls it with self = Money(5), other = Money(7)"], ["3", "isinstance check passes; builds Money(12)"], ["4", "The new object is the value of the expression"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A Vector with +, scalar *, equality and a readable form.", code: `class Vector:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n    def __mul__(self, k):\n        return Vector(self.x * k, self.y * k)\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return (self.x, self.y) == (other.x, other.y)\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f"Vector({self.x}, {self.y})"\n\nv = Vector(1, 2) + Vector(3, 4)\nprint(v, v * 3, v == Vector(4, 6))`, out: `Vector(4, 6) Vector(12, 18) True` },
  mistakes: ["Modifying <code>self</code> inside <code>__add__</code>.", "Defining <code>__eq__</code> but not <code>__hash__</code> and then using objects in a set.", "Raising TypeError instead of returning NotImplemented.", "Overusing magic methods for clever but surprising behaviour.", "Forgetting <code>__repr__</code> so debugging output is useless."],
  practices: ["Make operators behave as users expect.", "Always provide <code>__repr__</code>.", "Use <code>total_ordering</code> to avoid writing all comparisons.", "Prefer plain methods when an operator would be unclear."],
  interview: ["What is polymorphism? What is duck typing?", "What is operator overloading?", "What does __eq__ without __hash__ do?", "How do you emulate method overloading in Python?"],
  practiceQs: ["Implement __len__ for a Playlist.", "Add __sub__ to Money.", "Why does Python return NotImplemented?", "Make a class instance callable."],
  revision: ["Polymorphism: same call, different behaviour.", "Dunder methods connect objects to operators and built-ins.", "__eq__ + __hash__; total_ordering.", "Overloading via defaults, *args, singledispatch."],
  hinglish: "Polymorphism ka matlab ek hi call alag objects par alag kaam kare. __add__, __len__, __eq__ jaise special methods se hamare objects +, len(), == jaise operators ke saath kaam karte hain. __eq__ likho to __hash__ bhi likho.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("Which special method makes len(obj) work?", ["__size__", "__len__", "__length__", "__count__"], 1, "len calls __len__."),
    mcq("What does v1 + v2 call?", ["v1.add(v2)", "v1.__add__(v2)", "v2.__add__(v1) always", "add(v1, v2)"], 1, "The left operand's __add__ is tried first."),
    mcq("What should __add__ return for an unsupported operand type?", ["None", "NotImplemented", "False", "It should raise ValueError"], 1, "It lets Python try the reflected operation."),
    mcq("What problem appears if a class defines __eq__ but not __hash__?", ["Instances become unhashable", "Instances cannot be compared", "Class cannot be inherited", "Nothing"], 0, "Python sets __hash__ to None."),
    mcq("What is duck typing?", ["Checking types before calling", "Using any object that has the needed methods", "A type of loop", "Static typing"], 1, "Behaviour matters, not the declared type.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Vector arithmetic", "Define a Vector class with __add__ and __repr__ (the form Vector(x, y)). Read x1 y1 on the first line and x2 y2 on the second, and print the sum of the two vectors.", "class Vector:\n    pass\n\nx1, y1 = map(int, input().split())\nx2, y2 = map(int, input().split())\n# print Vector(x1, y1) + Vector(x2, y2)\n", `class Vector:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f"Vector({self.x}, {self.y})"\n\nx1, y1 = map(int, input().split())\nx2, y2 = map(int, input().split())\nprint(Vector(x1, y1) + Vector(x2, y2))`, [["1 2\n3 4", "Vector(4, 6)"], ["0 0\n-1 5", "Vector(-1, 5)"]], [["1 2\n3 4", "Vector(4, 6)"], ["0 0\n-1 5", "Vector(-1, 5)"], ["10 10\n10 10", "Vector(20, 20)"], ["-3 -4\n3 4", "Vector(0, 0)"]])],
});

