lessons.push({
  title: "Abstraction, Abstract Base Classes, Composition and Dataclasses",
  minutes: 55,
  learn: ["Hide details behind a clear interface with abstract base classes", "Choose composition over inheritance when the relation is has-a", "Write concise data classes with @dataclass", "Model a Library, Online Shop and Vehicle fleet"],
  why: "Abstraction lets you program against what something <em>does</em> rather than how. Composition keeps designs flexible. Dataclasses remove the boilerplate of classes that mostly hold data.",
  realWorld: "A <code>Library</code> has many <code>Book</code> objects; a payment system accepts any <code>PaymentMethod</code> that can <code>pay(amount)</code>; an API response model is a dataclass.",
  simple: "An <strong>abstract class</strong> declares methods that every child must provide but does not implement them. <strong>Composition</strong> builds big objects from smaller ones (a Library <em>has</em> Books). A <strong>dataclass</strong> writes <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code> and <code>__eq__</code> for you.",
  detail: `<h3>Abstract base classes (ABC)</h3>
<pre style="background:#16181c;color:#d8dee9;padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self): ...</code></pre>
<p>You cannot create a <code>Shape()</code>; a subclass must implement every abstract method before it can be instantiated. This guarantees the interface and documents it. (Python also has <code>typing.Protocol</code> for structural typing, Module 11.)</p>
<h3>Composition versus inheritance</h3>
${table(["", "Inheritance (is-a)", "Composition (has-a)"], [["Example", "<code>Car(Vehicle)</code>", "<code>Car</code> has an <code>Engine</code>"], ["Coupling", "tight: child depends on the parent's internals", "loose: depends on the other object's interface"], ["Change at run time", "no", "yes: swap the engine"], ["Rule of thumb", "only for true specialisation", "the default choice"]])}
<h3>dataclasses</h3>
<p><code>@dataclass</code> generates <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code> and <code>__eq__</code> from annotated fields. Options: <code>frozen=True</code> (immutable and hashable), <code>order=True</code> (comparisons), <code>slots=True</code>, <code>kw_only=True</code> (Python 3.10+). Mutable defaults need <code>field(default_factory=list)</code>. <code>__post_init__</code> runs after the generated <code>__init__</code> for validation.</p>
<h3>Abstraction levels</h3>
<p>Good abstractions expose a small, stable interface and hide the rest: callers use <code>library.borrow(book_id, member)</code> and never touch the internal dictionaries.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Book:\n    title: str\n    copies: int = 1\n    tags: list = field(default_factory=list)`, note: "Annotated fields become constructor parameters." },
  diagram: graph("Abstraction and composition", "Abstract Shape with Circle and Rectangle implementing it; a Drawing object holds a list of shapes (composition).", 640, 250,
    [{ id: "s", k: "term", t: "Shape (abstract)\narea()", x: 320, y: 40, w: 210, h: 56 }, { id: "c", k: "proc", t: "Circle\narea()", x: 170, y: 140, w: 140, h: 48 }, { id: "r", k: "proc", t: "Rectangle\narea()", x: 470, y: 140, w: 140, h: 48 }, { id: "d", k: "io", t: "Drawing  has a list of Shapes", x: 320, y: 225, w: 280, h: 40 }],
    [{ from: "c", to: "s" }, { from: "r", to: "s" }, { from: "d", to: "c" }, { from: "d", to: "r" }]),
  example: { code: `from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nimport math\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self): ...\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, r):\n        self.r = r\n    def area(self):\n        return math.pi * self.r ** 2\n\nclass Rectangle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, w, h):\n        self.w, self.h = w, h\n    def area(self):\n        return self.w * self.h\n\ntry:\n    Shape()\nexcept TypeError:\n    print("cannot instantiate an abstract class")\nprint(round(sum(s.area() for s in [Circle(1), Rectangle(2, 3)]), 2))\n\n@dataclass\nclass Book:\n    title: str\n    copies: int = 1\n    tags: list = field(default_factory=list)\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True, order=True)\nclass Version:\n    major: int\n    minor: int = 0\n\nb = Book("Python")\nprint(b, b == Book("Python"))\nprint(Version(1, 2) < Version(1, 10), Version(2) == Version(2, 0))\n\nclass Library:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.books = {}\n    def add(self, book):\n        self.books[book.title] = book\n    def borrow(self, title):\n        book = self.books.get(title)\n        if book is None or book.copies == 0:\n            return False\n        book.copies -= 1\n        return True\n\nlib = Library()\nlib.add(Book("Python", 1))\nprint(lib.borrow("Python"), lib.borrow("Python"), lib.borrow("Java"))`, out: `cannot instantiate an abstract class\n9.14\nBook(title='Python', copies=1, tags=[]) True\nTrue True\nTrue False False` },
  lineByLine: [["class Shape(ABC) with @abstractmethod", "A contract: every shape must provide area()."], ["Shape()", "TypeError: abstract classes cannot be instantiated."], ["sum(s.area() for s in [...])", "Polymorphism: any Shape works."], ["@dataclass class Book", "Gets __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ automatically."], ["field(default_factory=list)", "A new list per object, avoiding the shared-default trap."], ["frozen=True, order=True", "Immutable, hashable and comparable field by field."], ["class Library", "Composition: it holds Book objects and exposes borrow()."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "<code>lib.borrow(\"Python\")</code> called twice:", headers: ["Call", "copies before", "Result", "copies after"], rows: [["1st", "1", "True", "0"], ["2nd", "0", "False (none left)", "0"], ["borrow(\"Java\")", "-", "False (not found)", "-"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "An online shop: abstract payment methods are interchangeable, an Order is composed of items.", code: `from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nclass Payment(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def pay(self, amount): ...\n\nclass Upi(Payment):\n    def pay(self, amount):\n        return f"UPI paid {amount}"\n\nclass Card(Payment):\n    def pay(self, amount):\n        return f"Card paid {amount + amount // 50}"\n\n@dataclass\nclass Item:\n    name: str\n    price: int\n\nclass Order:\n    def __init__(self, items):\n        self.items = items\n    def total(self):\n        return sum(i.price for i in self.items)\n    def checkout(self, method):\n        return method.pay(self.total())\n\norder = Order([Item("pen", 10), Item("bag", 490)])\nprint(order.checkout(Upi()))\nprint(order.checkout(Card()))`, out: `UPI paid 500\nCard paid 510` },
  mistakes: ["Instantiating an abstract class.", "Forgetting to implement every abstract method in the child.", "Using a mutable default in a dataclass (it raises an error unless you use default_factory).", "Using inheritance where the relation is has-a.", "Putting business rules into dataclasses that should only hold data."],
  practices: ["Program to an interface (the abstract class), not to a concrete class.", "Prefer composition; use inheritance for true specialisation.", "Use dataclasses for records and value objects; use <code>frozen=True</code> when the data should not change."],
  interview: ["What is an abstract class?", "Composition vs inheritance?", "What does @dataclass generate?", "Why can't you instantiate an ABC?"],
  practiceQs: ["Design a Vehicle ABC with wheels() and a Car and Bike.", "Create a frozen dataclass Point and use it as a dictionary key.", "Why would you give Library a list of books instead of subclassing list?", "What does field(default_factory=dict) solve?"],
  revision: ["ABC + @abstractmethod = enforced interface.", "has-a → composition; is-a → inheritance.", "@dataclass: init, repr, eq; frozen, order, slots.", "default_factory for mutable defaults."],
  hinglish: "Abstract class ek contract hai jisme kuch methods likhna zaroori hota hai, uska object seedha nahi ban sakta. Composition mein ek object doosre objects ko rakhta hai (Library has Books). @dataclass se __init__, __repr__ aur __eq__ apne aap ban jaate hain.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What happens when you instantiate a class with an unimplemented abstract method?", ["Works normally", "TypeError", "ValueError", "Returns None"], 1, "The class is abstract."),
    mcq("A Library that holds Book objects is an example of:", ["Inheritance", "Composition", "Polymorphism only", "Encapsulation only"], 1, "It has-a collection of books."),
    mcq("What does @dataclass(frozen=True) provide?", ["Immutable instances", "Faster loops", "Abstract methods", "Class variables"], 0, "Assignments to fields raise an error, and instances become hashable."),
    mcq("How do you give a dataclass field an empty list default?", ["tags: list = []", "tags: list = field(default_factory=list)", "tags = list", "tags: list = None only"], 1, "Mutable defaults need a factory."),
    mcq("Which statement is true for @abstractmethod?", ["Subclasses must override it before instantiation", "It makes the method private", "It is required for every method", "It speeds up code"], 0, "It defines a mandatory interface.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Total area of shapes", "Standard input holds one shape per line: circle R or rect W H. Using an abstract Shape class with an area() method and two subclasses, print the total area of all shapes rounded to 2 decimals.", "import sys, math\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\n# add Circle and Rectangle, read the lines from sys.stdin and print the total\n", `import sys, math\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, r):\n        self.r = r\n    def area(self):\n        return math.pi * self.r ** 2\n\nclass Rectangle(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, w, h):\n        self.w, self.h = w, h\n    def area(self):\n        return self.w * self.h\n\nshapes = []\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    parts = line.split()\n    if not parts:\n        continue\n    if parts[0] == "circle":\n        shapes.append(Circle(float(parts[1])))\n    else:\n        shapes.append(Rectangle(float(parts[1]), float(parts[2])))\nprint(round(sum(s.area() for s in shapes), 2))`, [["circle 2\nrect 3 4", "24.57"], ["rect 2 5", "10.0"]], [["circle 2\nrect 3 4", "24.57"], ["rect 2 5", "10.0"], ["circle 1", "3.14"], ["circle 1\ncircle 1\nrect 1 1", "7.28"]])],
});

